 this study reviewed  over 50 publications dealing with the  analysis of microplastics and they  didn't choose  complicated matrices like sewage or fish  tissues this was done on fresh water and  drinking water samples some of the  cleanest sample matrices one would think  they could encounter  in the review of these 50 research  articles  the conclusion was contamination is  unavoidable  in only 32 of the 50 studies were  procedural blanks analyzed  and then only  three of the 50 samples were procedural  spikes used this lack of data integrity  and method validation calls into  question some of the results  i remember early on in my graduate  career with bonner denton he expressed  this phrase often  bad data is worse than no data at all it  impedes your decision-making abilities  it exacerbates the problem you're  attempting to study  it can negatively impact your reputation  and it could have severe cost  implications  which brings us to the current state of  our research effort  chronicled over the last three years in  total we chose these 13 analytical  methods for the basis of our study  one because  being spectroscopist by nature  these ftir and raman spectroscopy  methods allowed for positive  identification against known libraries  of compounds  they also allowed for visual  identification of particles which aided  in quantification now there are some  research groups out there that prefer gc  pyrolysis but that's a destructive  technique  which while it does positively identify  the polymer type it gives you a total  mass number  uh it it's a destructive technique and  so you lose out all the finer aspects as  far as the number of particles and the  size distribution of particles  in particular  consider the cost of these different  analytical methods they range in price  wildly from two hundred and twenty  dollars a sample to over four thousand  dollars for a sample  this is due to the amount of time  required to prepare the sample for  analysis and also the uh and also the  length of time for analysis on the  equipment  if you're using traditional micro ftir  or micro raman using a rastering type  stage  your analysis times could be multiple  days  if you're using a focal plane array  detector your analysis times can easily  be 24 hours or greater  so starting at the beginning  with sample collection  you'll see that most sample collection  techniques relied on two methodologies  either sieving of a sample  allowing you to get specific size  fractions or filtering a sample  i prefer sieving sieving you can  purchase sieves with different  poor diameters  whereas filters tend to often clog and  be problematic  and the sieves can be stacked from  larger diameter down to smaller diameter  allowing to collect specific fragments  or particle size and this may be very  beneficial to some of these bottled  water  distributions in determining where in  the process are the particles coming are  they coming from the actual blow  injection of the bottles are they coming  from the screw on lids or is there  delamination of the polymeric membranes  in the ro systems  there was also considerable  variation in the sample size used for  each of these studies  collecting a representative sample is  absolutely necessary  especially when trying to extrapolate  and quantify for larger operations  for example if you're trying to  characterize  the discharge from a  let's say a 20 million gallon a day  wastewater treatment facility and the  amount of polymers that are discharged  the environment you want to capture the  entire diurnal pattern throughout the  day so a 24-hour composite is probably  the best route  and you'll see that some of these  studies used as small as 700 mils to  just over a liter  now here's the problem if you're using  such a small sample volume  and you do have natural contamination  either in your sample collection or your  sample preparation or your sample  preparation is so vigorous that it  breaks down particles into multiple  particle components  when you try to extrapolate that  one liter to a 20 million gallon you're  going to vastly overestimate the number  of particles so there's a definitely a  need for large volumes for  representative consistency  because we are spectroscopists we chose  to stick with the  the analytical methods typically  involved around micro ftir micro ramen  but there was one that looked at  stereoscopic visual identification  these visual methods of identification  are grossly  inaccurate they're unable to distinguish  one plastic polymer from another  we've even seen some where they use a  hot pin to touch the plastic particle  and see if it melts if it does well then  it's confirmed plastics but you know  really that's that has all the  scientific integrity of a coin toss and  should be discarded we need these  methods that we can compare the plastics  against a known library  and this is where gc mass spectroscopy  works as well but then you lose the  value of sample particle size and number  distribution  so the following slides  depict the questions that epa posed of  us in developing this method our goal  was to add greater analytical robustness  and greater data integrity  so in order for a method to be robust  we need standardization and  reproducibility that reproducibility has  to occur at the sample collection sample  preparation and throughout sample  analysis  i'm assuming most of the people  listening are laboratory personnel  and therefore you're very accustomed to  using sop standard operating procedures  for  sample collection preparation and  analysis  and that's what we're trying to  establish here reproducible methods that  would  tender the same type of results  regardless of which lab or who was doing  the  preparation and analysis  so the primary goal for sample  collection is one that it's  straightforward it's simple to follow  and it's reproducive as possible  these were the goals for any analytical  sampling methodology  and because we were using wastewater  matrices  epa selected us because we're a  wastewater utility and they wanted to  start with one of the more difficult  sample preparation and difficult  matrices available if it would work in  wastewater  then it was undoubtedly going to work in  surface waters and drinking water  samples  and so  the cartoon on the left depicts some of  the necessary components you'll need to  do this  because most the tankage in a wastewater  treatment plant are below grade you're  going to need a pump  capable of pumping with considerable  head 10 to 20 feet  the flow rate we selected was three  gallons per minute that was selected so  as not to overwhelm the sieve stack now  if you're doing influent you're probably  going to have a lot of debris and  cellulose materials lots of toilet paper  and influence and they'll tend to clog  up the sieve stack and so we do a  stacked set of sieves that we can pull  out particular fractions 300 micron  fraction 100 micron fractions if you're  going over three gallons a minute  depending on how dirty the insulin is  you can quickly overwhelm those sieves  so having flow control over the volume  is definitely necessary we chose  stainless steel transfer tubing for  everything we didn't want nylon ptfe or  any type of materials that could uh  possibly add contaminants to our sample  and be miscounted  a programmable timer now  at three gallons per minute in one of  our influence samples will probably bind  up and clog that screen mesh in about  three hours  and so i would typically set my timer  for two and a half hour intervals we'd  go out collect the material from the  sieve  re-initiate the pump for another two and  a half hours and we would do that over a  24-hour period so we're getting a  composite sample over a 24-hour period  sieve stacks you can get in various uh  porosity diameters  and then some type of a  collection sieve here it's showing a  plastic bucket but we took a regular uh  aluminum bucket drilled holes in the  bottom that contained all the sieves in  it  one other key feature that you should  consider is an inline flow meter inline  flow meter will help you dial in the  flow control of the volume going to the  sieves  that'll avoid overburdening them but  also a flow controller that also  is a totalizer and records total volume  process  that's very handy and so the ideal  features would be something that's easy  to set up and calibrate something allows  flow control  something that records total volume  you can use peristaltic or or  submersible pumps  and fractionating sieves  so the photograph on the left is of a  typical influence sample you'll see  large debris  large mat largely toilet paper that's  binding on the filter while the one on  the right that's a much finer mesh  that's probably about a 25 micron sieve  and it's free of debris still has some  shallows material but a lot of algal  material thing a lot of our tanks are  open exposed daylight so we get a lot of  algal buildup in there and that's what  you see on the sieve itself  so the real challenge comes in the  sample preparation and again  the sop that we're trying to develop  needs to be easy to replicate  needs to address interferences  and minimize the loss of particles one  item not on here is minimize the  addition of particles if you have an  aggressive sample preparation scheme  and it's overly aggressive even just  stirring the sample overly aggressive  one particle could break into two three  multiple particles and then numerically  you're over counting those particles so  we want to keep that in mind as well  so one of the test methods i didn't  highlight on the 13 methods that we  evaluated was this one by loader in  2017.  it's the universal enzymatic  purification protocol  this is a very elegant protocol because  it addresses a variety of of  interference that one may encounter  especially in wastewater matrices  it addresses proteins fats oils and  greases  toilet paper cellulose  has a wet peroxide oxidation  chitinase that  that is a lot of the you're going to see  bug fragments that are in sewage  as well so we chose it based on these  three key characteristics  and in a slightly different order almost  all the methodologies we encountered use  some form of wet oxidation with peroxide  and a few of them used  density separations using either zinc  chloride or a calcium chloride  and when dealing with wastewater  cellulose is a is a huge bird it's  probably 90 95 of the problem that  you're trying to address is the  interference from cellulose and so  these three steps we took and we chose  to improve upon them because looking at  here look at the amount of time it was  required to do this analysis it ranged  from  well over a week up to 15 days to  prepare early steps  keep in mind each one of these steps  you're adding something to the sample  it's exposed to your lab environment so  if contamination is ubiquitous if it's  everywhere you're going to contaminate  that sample at every step along the way  and so we chose these three steps  outlined in red  but going away from the enzymatic  protocol we needed faster turnaround  time and to avoid contamination to make  this a viable test for most laboratories  and we also chose to implement some type  of protocol to validate our study so we  had method spikes included as well so  this was the samples that we analyzed  using the loader protocol with the  enzymatic  buffers  and  you'll see there is some cellulosic  debris still present in the sample but  it's  probably 99 removed from what it was in  the original wastewater sample you see  these large particles these were  obviously an influence sample and we  spiked them with two different types of  polymers  i've highlighted those here the red  polyethylene polymers these were 700 or  70 excuse me 70 to 120 microns  and the larger particles were another  polypropylene that was in the 200 micron  range in size and so we spiked with two  different type of polymers  and when you visually look at them under  a microscope like this if the particles  are broken then you can  it leads to overestimation but it tells  you that your  preparation technique is probably too  aggressive  and if you don't recover your particles  you're probably  assured that you're losing material  throughout your process as well and so  this was very valuable for us  our final method selection uses those  three key features that we found common  to most analytical methods but in a  slightly different order we chose as our  first stage to do the wet oxidation  almost 90 percent of the research  articles use some form of wet oxidation  and we find that extremely valuable  because  for one it oxidizes a lot of the organic  matter and the easily oxidizable carbon  and so it's a form of disinfecting the  sample and protecting the laboratory  staff  before moving forward  we do this with a fenton reagent which  is a 30 peroxide with an iron catalyst  this will destroy pathogens it oxidizes  the organic matter in the sample  rendering it much cleaner especially in  those effluent samples where you have a  lot of algal  material  and it also releases materials from the  plastic surface  so one of the things that epa was  concerned about was the amount of  biofilm and co-contaminants that may be  deposited on a sample particle plastic  particle  and so when you think of that we  commonly see  pcbs and other type of materials that  are bound to plastics and if these are  in an ocean or in a lake or a waterway  the aquatic species will then come along  eat those plastics ingest those coke  contaminants as well and so that's why  it's critical for us to know the shape  and size of the particle to estimate how  much coke contaminants are on there as  well and the fenton region releases that  biofilm and those contaminants from the  plastic surface  the next step was the use of a  schweizer's region  in place of the cellulase digestion  in the loader method they used a  cellulase digestion which proved quite  effective but it was very timely often  required up to three days to remove  toilet uh the cellulose  toilet paper from the influence samples  and so schweizer's reagent is actually a  copper ammonia complex and it's almost  like  something a magician you would use you  add the schweizer's reagent to the  sample it instantly  breaks down the cellulose and allows us  to then take that same sample run it  through the corresponding sieve and the  cellulose is gone we then extract those  particles right off the sieve a second  time it's a rapid reaction it eliminates  cellulose it greatly minimizes sample  preparation time and it avoids  contamination  the third  key step  was density separations throughout the  literature we'll see a lot of  laboratories using either a zinc  chloride or calcium chloride solution  uh at a concentration that makes it to a  density of 1.4 to 1.7 grams per mil  in effect the heavier inorganic type  material sinks to the bottom the  plastics float to the polymer plastic  polymers float to the top then those are  then skimmed off and  analyzed  one problem that we note with that  is you'll see a lot of those plastic  particles that float to the top want to  adhere to the edge of the container  which can be problematic  and so for our studies we chose to use a  centrifuge rather than  floating the material to the top and  i'll describe that briefly for you  so here's the way the procedure would  typically go and depending on the sample  matrices you're using all of these steps  may not be necessary  so this is the worst case scenario using  waste water influence we'll do a wet  peroxide with a fetan reagent for an  hour that's going to decontaminate the  sample and remove all the easily  oxidizable carbon and lift any biofilm  off of the plastic particles themselves  rendering them  much  a much cleaner surface for spectroscopy  analysis  we would 