vidual particles they will also  allow a  particle size distribution which is  going to be  a beneficial feature  and provided you have a focal plane  array or some instrument that's able to  rapidly scan the slide the results  should be fast and reproducible  so after working with microrahmann  method for a good part of a year  we were able to obtain  agilent ldr 8700 as a chemical imaging  system that was originally designed for  pharmaceutical analysis  however it has demonstrated itself be  very beneficial for  particle analysis as well  so the first step is to take that slide  that we previously plated we insert it  into the instrument and the first thing  we select is the  relative sensitivity  we would  pull up a library the microplastics  library that we've assembled has common  components such as polyethylene  polypropylene  ldpe hdpe  teflon and other common rubbers and  different types of plastic polymers  we then select the sensitivity for this  slide this is influence sample so i've  only selected to analyze for particles  larger than 100 micron and smaller than  5000 micro now if we were looking at  effluent samples we may change this to  look at particles from  20 micron up to maybe 300 microns  this was material obtained on a 500  micron sieve  and  this area that's under analysis  this probably took it roughly an hour to  analyze  and it's  probably about 1 6 of the slide  and you'll see the number of particles  that were analyzed and identified on  this  here's the most important part  every particle that we've analyzed you  get an optical image  and then you get the infrared image  along with  the spectrum and the reference spectrum  overlaid  and you also get the benefit of the  diameter and area of the particle and  this is something that epa was very  interested in for establishing what the  co-contaminant  loading may be on these particles  and so these were a polystyrene particle  here you see the data  for that entire slide you see the number  count  and the number of particles that were  detected what percentage that they  represent of the total particles  identified  and for any particles that are  unidentified that are unknown you can  always go back and manually  analyze them with an ftir and maybe look  at against different libraries now this  is just the natural  microplastics library which you  typically see here we still have  cellulose on screen because we know  we're dealing with wastewater matrices  so we look for that but here's some  normal  sample analysis  and it tells you what the particle size  range was for each of those particles so  for this wastewater influent we'll see  that almost 95 of all of our particles  are in the 20 to 50 micron range with  the second tier being the 50 to 100  micron range  very valuable information we're able to  acquire  so thank you for your time today  here's my contact information if any of  you would like to get in touch with me  if you have any suggestions for  improvements please feel free to reach  out to me thank you  you
 

